Single column tests of the RegCM physics schemes
================================================

The programs here call one physics scheme through its model entry point
on a set of atmospheric columns, without the dynamical core. They are
regression tests ("make check") and a quick way to look at the response
of a scheme to its parameters. For now: the Tiedtke cumulus scheme
(icup = 5) and the Holtslag boundary layer scheme (ibltyp = 1), alone
and together in a time integration (scm_coupled).

Files
-----
  scm_tiedtke.F90     driver of mod_cu_tiedtke (tiedtkedrv), MOLOCH setup
                      (idynamic = 3, NoTo microphysics, no chemistry)
  scm_tiedtke.py      Python wrapper: run, summary, sweep, plot, compare
  scm_holtslag.F90    driver of mod_pbl_holtbl (holtbl), MOLOCH setup;
                      one call, or nsteps time steps with fixed surface
                      fluxes and the initial wind as geostrophic wind
  scm_holtslag.py     Python wrapper, as scm_tiedtke.py
  scm_coupled.F90     Holtslag + Tiedtke + NoTo + CCM radiation
                      integrated together in time with prescribed large
                      scale forcing
  scm_coupled.py      its Python wrapper: run, summary, sweep, compare
  scm_amazon.txt      Amazon case of scm_coupled: initial profiles,
  scm_amazon_target.txt   relaxation targets (0, 6, 12, 18 UTC),
  scm_amazon_era5.txt     ERA5 rain and 10 m wind by local hour
  scm_itcz.txt, scm_itcz_target.txt, scm_itcz_era5.txt
                      ocean case of scm_coupled (Atlantic ITCZ, South
                      Atlantic trades), same layout
  make_amazon_case.py builds the files of a case from ERA5
                      (--case amazon or itcz)
  scm_ocean.F90       ocean surface flux schemes on samples of the
                      lowest level and the surface
  scm_ocean.py        evaluation of all the schemes and options
  scm_profiles.txt    the twelve test columns (from ERA5, see below)
  make_profiles.py    builds scm_profiles.txt from ERA5
  run_scm_tests.sh    the "make check" test
  reference/          reference outputs of the check

Test columns
------------
Six columns per hemisphere, land and ocean, on the 32 MOLOCH levels of
the South America test:
  Sahel, India, Great Plains (land), West Pacific warm pool, California
  stratocumulus, North Atlantic trades (ocean);
  Amazon, Congo, central Australia (land), SPCZ, South East Atlantic
  stratocumulus, South Atlantic trades (ocean).
The deep convection columns are taken at the 6-hourly time of September
2023 with the largest ERA5 convective rain at the point, the others on
2023-09-15. Surface fluxes are ERA5 monthly means (land: scaled by a half
sine diurnal cycle at the local time), the PBL height a bulk Richardson
number diagnosis. The friction velocity is set by the driver from the
wind of the lowest level (neutral log law, z0 0.1 m on land, 1 mm on the
ocean). The input tendencies are zero, as in the model where
the cumulus scheme is the first physics call. ERA5 CAPE and convective
rain rate are listed as a reference, not as a target.

GATE test (Tiedtke)
-------------------
testgate holds the 161 three-hourly GATE Phase III soundings (30 August
to 19 September 1974, A/B array near 8.5N 23.5W) on 41 pressure levels,
with the observed apparent heat source Q1, moisture sink Q2, radiative
heating QR and the horizontal advection of T and q (ADVT, ADVQ: the
vertical advection is not included and is computed from omega).
make_gate_profiles.py turns them into a profile file for the drivers
and a file with the observed Q1, Q2, QR:

  ./make_gate_profiles.py -o scm_gate.txt     forcing advrad (default)
  ./make_gate_profiles.py --forcing none -o scm_gate_none.txt

The input tendencies are the large scale forcing: horizontal plus
vertical advection (adv), plus QR (advrad), or zero (none, as in RegCM
where cumulus is the first physics call). The soundings have no surface
fluxes (constant --shf 10, --lhf 125 W/m2, assumed) and no PBL height
(bulk Richardson diagnosis). One call on each sounding is a
semi-prognostic test: the wrapper compares the scheme heating and drying
with Q1-QR and Q2 (detrained condensate counted as evaporated, as in the
observed budget) and the rain with the budget precipitation
E + cp/L int Q2 dp/g:

  ./scm_tiedtke.py --profiles scm_gate.txt --gate scm_gate_obs.txt

"make check" runs the 8 soundings of scm_gate_check.txt (numbers 1, 21,
..., 141, advrad forcing) against reference/scm_tiedtke_gate.out.

Holtslag test
-------------
Same columns. The driver computes from the profiles what the model
derives from its state: potential temperature, surface air density, the
Coriolis parameter, the surface drag rho u*^2/V1 (u* as above) and a
ground temperature with the potential temperature of the lowest level
(used only with ifaholtth10 /= 2). ricr and kmxpbl are set as in the
model. The namelist has &scmparam (infile, outfile, dtpbl, nsteps) and
&holtslagparam. "make check" uses one call (nsteps = 1); with nsteps > 1
the columns evolve with the PBL tendencies only, no radiation, advection
or subsidence, so the boundary layer deepens under the surface fluxes:

  ./scm_holtslag.py                           one call, summary table
  ./scm_holtslag.py --set nsteps=90           6 hours at dtpbl = 240 s
  ./scm_holtslag.py --sweep ricr_ocn=0.25,0.275,0.3
  ./scm_holtslag.py --set nsteps=90 --plot holtslag.png

The summary lists the PBL height against the ERA5 diagnosis of the
profile file, and the column heating and moistening of the first call,
which equal the surface sensible and latent heat fluxes. Output per
level: tendencies of the first call (T, q, qc, u, v) and the changes of
T, q, u, v after nsteps. The comparison is exact for the PBL top level.

Coupled test
------------
scm_coupled calls tiedtkedrv, the SUBEX cloud fraction and nogtom (NoTo),
and holtbl every dtstep (300 s) on the same state, as the MOLOCH core
does, and advances the state with their
tendencies plus the large scale forcing: the input tendencies of the
profile file (here a radiative cooling of 1.5 K/day), the vertical
advection by its omega (ERA5 monthly mean), and a relaxation of T and q
with time scale taurelax (1 day) to the ERA5 monthly mean profiles at
0, 6, 12, 18 UTC (tgtfile), standing for the horizontal advection. Land
surface fluxes follow a half sine in local time with the ERA5 daily
means (small night values). Ocean columns (an SST in the profile file)
get the fluxes, drag and friction velocity from the ocean scheme of the
model (iocnflux 1 Zeng with izengrough, 2 COARE 3.5; 0 the prescribed
ERA5 means) on the lowest level and the SST, every step. NoTo gets the
cumulus tendencies and detrained condensate, the large scale omega and
the input T tendency as radiative heating, carries cloud water, ice,
rain and snow (not advected) and gives the large scale rain; its
parameters are in &microparam and &cldparam (rh0oce, rh0land, ...).
With lnoto = .false. there is no microphysics: detrained condensate
evaporates, supersaturation rains out (large scale rain). Cloud water
and ice are also passed to Tiedtke and Holtslag (which diffuses them).

Radiation (irad = 1, default; 0 keeps the input T tendency of the
profile file as radiative heating): the CCM scheme of the model
(colmod3) every dtrad (1800 s), absorptivities every dtabem (64800 s),
sun of the time of the step, GHG of the built in table of scenario
(RCP4.5), built in ozone, no aerosol, clouds as in the model (Tiedtke
convective cloud overlapped with the NoTo cloud), ocean albedo of the
model or albland (0.13) on land. Its heating replaces the input T
tendency, its net surface radiation drives the COARE cool skin.

The output levels also have the NoTo tendencies, cloud water + ice, the
cloud fraction seen by the radiation and the radiative heating.

Options of &scmparam beyond the forcing above:
  tauwind, windfac   winds relax to windfac times the target winds in
                     tauwind (0, default: Coriolis around the initial
                     winds times windfac)
  tauwtg, zwtg       weak temperature gradient: above zwtg (2000 m) and
                     below 100 hPa the large scale omega is the one that
                     brings T back to the target in tauwtg, (Tr - T) /
                     (tauwtg S) with S the static stability of the
                     target; linear in height below zwtg; there T is not
                     relaxed; converging flow brings in air with the
                     target humidity. 0 (default): the omega of the
                     profile file
  ocnsw, ocnlw       net shortwave (daily mean of a half sine) and
                     longwave radiation at the sea surface (COARE cool
                     skin)
With WTG the column makes its own ascent or subsidence: a test of
whether the convective heating of the schemes can hold the observed
temperature profile against the radiative cooling.

The Amazon case (make_amazon_case.py) has two boxes, NW Amazon (5S-5N,
75-60W) and S Amazon (12-5S, 65-50W), September 2023. Rain is
intermittent, so the wrapper averages from the second day on; over 30
days (its default) the mean daily rain is stable to about 0.1 mm/day:

  ./scm_coupled.py                            30 days, rain and diurnal
                                              cycle against ERA5
  ./scm_coupled.py --set lmfcapebl=.false.
  ./scm_coupled.py --sweep zhnew_fac=0.0,0.25,0.5
  ./scm_coupled.py --profiles scm_itcz.txt --set tauwind=10800 \
                   --sweep tauwtg=0,10800

The ITCZ case (--case itcz) has the ocean points of the Atlantic ITCZ
(2-10N, 45-25W) and of the South Atlantic trades (25-5S, 30-10W), with
the monthly mean SST. For ocean columns the summary also gives the mean
surface fluxes and lowest level wind against ERA5, and the mean omega
at 850, 700, 500, 300 hPa against the input one.

"make check" runs one day of each case against
reference/scm_coupled_amazon.out and scm_coupled_itcz.out (the ITCZ one
with WTG and wind relaxation). The small differences of another
compiler or other flags grow into different rain events: the reference
holds only for the build it was made with. To update one, copy
scm_output/scm_coupled_<case>/scm_coupled_<case>.out of a "make check"
run and set its "# profiles:" and "# targets:" lines to the file names
without the directory.

Ocean flux test
---------------
scm_ocean calls one ocean surface flux scheme (Zeng iocnflux 1 with
izengrough, COARE 3.5 iocnflux 2) through its model entry point on a
list of samples of the lowest model level and the surface (height, wind,
T, mixing ratio, pressure, surface pressure, bulk SST, PBL height, net
radiation), on open water. scm_ocean.py runs every scheme and option on
the same samples and compares them with the NOAA COARE 3.5 algorithm
(--coare: the Python/COARE3.5 directory of
https://github.com/NOAA-PSL/COARE-algorithm), or with the default Zeng
configuration without it:

  ./scm_ocean.py curves --coare DIR     transfer coefficients against
                                        the wind, fluxes against the
                                        air-sea temperature difference
  ./scm_ocean.py era5 --coare DIR       ERA5 September 2023 states (1000
                                        hPa level, daily SST) over the
                                        South America ocean, box means
                                        against the ERA5 monthly fluxes

Running
-------
"make check" in this directory builds scm_tiedtke and runs it with the
default settings, then compares the output with reference/ if python3 is
available. After a change of the scheme that is meant to change the
results, update the reference:

  ./scm_tiedtke.py --save reference/scm_tiedtke.out

then edit its "# profiles:" line to read "scm_profiles.txt".

The wrapper takes the same parameter names as the RegCM namelist
(&tiedtkeparam, and kfac_deep, kfac_shal, k2_const, iconvcf of
&cldparam), plus
dtcum and dxkm:

  ./scm_tiedtke.py                            defaults, summary table
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --list                     parameters used
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --set entrpen_ocn=1.0e-3 --set lmfwstar=.true.
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --sweep rprc_ocn=1.0e-3,1.4e-3,2.0e-3
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --plot tiedtke.png         profiles, needs matplotlib
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --compare reference/scm_tiedtke.out
  ./scm_tiedtke.py --check a.out b.out        compare two outputs

The comparison is exact for the cloud type, base and top, and within a
relative tolerance (--rtol, SCM_RTOL, default 1e-4) plus an absolute one
(--atol, SCM_ATOL, default 1e-5 times the largest value of the field in
the column) for the real values, to allow for compiler differences.

Output
------
Plain text: a header with the namelists used, then for each column a
COLUMN line (land flag, cloud type 0 none 1 deep 2 shallow 3 midlevel,
top and base level, rain and snow in mm/day, column heating and
moistening in W/m2, ERA5 CAPE and convective rain) and one line per
level, top to bottom: height, pressure, updraft mass flux, tendencies of
T, q, cloud liquid and ice, u and v (per day), convective cloud fraction,
detrained condensate and in cloud convective condensate for radiation
(g/m3).

Adding a scheme
---------------
Write scm_<scheme>.F90 reading the same scm_profiles.txt, add it to
check_PROGRAMS in Makefile.am and to the scheme list of run_scm_tests.sh,
and store its reference output in reference/scm_<scheme>.out.
